    {
     "id": "notes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Purpose.** See the period's outcome as the crossing of two curves in $(y, \\pi)$: the Phillips curve (supply) and the IS-MP curve (demand, with the central bank's rule built in). A supply shock shifts the Phillips curve up and the economy slides up and left along IS-MP: inflation up, output down. A tougher central bank flattens IS-MP, so the same shock raises inflation less and cuts output more.\n- **Model.** A view of [Three-Equation Macro Model](note:three-equation-macro-model); dashed curves are the no-shock, expectations-at-target references.\n- **Elements.** Panel `ismppc`: curves `pc0`, `ismp0`, `pc`, `ismp`; points `target`, `eq`. Readouts `pi_out`, `y_out`, `i_out`, `r_out`, `th_out`."
      }
     ],
     "words": 105,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\pi_t$",
      "$y_t$"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "notes-2",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Goal it supports.** `abs(pist - 3) < 0.01` with a supply shock of 3: met only at $\\beta_\\pi = 3$ on the 0.25 grid.\n- **Tests.** The notes' period-2 numbers and computed cases, Python."
      }
     ],
     "words": 34,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\beta_\\pi$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
